<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause

     Shared by Dockerfile.Stack and Dockerfile.Simulation. Both must use the
     same discovery settings: once the ROS stack runs in one container and O3DE
     in another, a multicast-enabled participant and a loopback-pinned one will
     discover each other only by luck.

     Multicast discovery is unreliable inside containers. Both containers run
     with network_mode: host, so they share the loopback interface and unicast
     discovery over it is deterministic. Split them across machines and this
     needs relaxing.

     The camera images are why the SharedMemory block is here. A 640x480 RGBA
     frame is 1.23 MB against a default net.core.rmem_max of 208 kB (416 kB
     once the kernel doubles it), and the publisher is BEST_EFFORT, so a
     fragment lost to the overrun is never retransmitted and takes the whole
     frame with it. Over UDP that measured camera_info at 12 Hz and image_color
     at 0.95 Hz - same component, same tick, same call, so size was the only
     variable, and nothing was logged.

     Shared memory sidesteps the socket: frames are handed over as segments
     brokered by iceoryx's RouDi, so there is no fragmentation, no socket
     buffer and no kernel tuning. Measured on an untuned host, 640x480 RGBA
     published at 12 Hz: 1.4 Hz received over UDP, 11.95 Hz over shared memory.
     Tuning the host instead reaches the same 12 Hz, but it is invisible state
     outside the repository, so a fresh machine silently reproduces the bug.

     This needs RouDi running, plus `ipc: host` AND a shared /tmp on every
     container that talks - the segments live in /dev/shm, which ipc: host
     covers, but clients register over a socket at /tmp/roudi and that path is
     compiled into iceoryx:
         docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.yml up -d roudi
     There is no fallback. A participant that cannot reach RouDi waits 60 s and
     then aborts the process with IPC_INTERFACE__REG_ROUDI_NOT_AVAILABLE, and
     that applies to every node using this config, not only the ones touching
     cameras. Hence depends_on in the compose file and a postStart hook in the
     devcontainer. The socket buffers below therefore never serve as a fallback
     for the images; they still matter for everything else, and only bite if
     the host has been tuned:
         sudo sysctl -w net.core.rmem_max=16777216 net.core.wmem_max=16777216
     `min` is a hard floor - Cyclone refuses to start if it cannot get it, and
     the launcher exits with "failed to increase socket receive buffer size" -
     so it stays low enough to always be satisfiable and the request goes in
     `max`, which the kernel silently clamps.

     Not done, both measured worse: MaxMessageSize=65500B to cut 686 fragments
     per frame to 15 (image_color fell to 0.25 Hz - coarser datagrams overrun a
     small buffer faster), and FastDDS, whose default 512 kB shared memory
     segment is too small for a frame, so it silently falls back to UDP and
     manages 1.0 Hz.
-->
<CycloneDDS xmlns="https://cdds.io/config">
  <Domain id="any">
    <General>
      <Interfaces><NetworkInterface address="127.0.0.1"/></Interfaces>
      <AllowMulticast>false</AllowMulticast>
    </General>
    <Discovery>
      <ParticipantIndex>auto</ParticipantIndex>
      <Peers><Peer address="localhost"/></Peers>
    </Discovery>
    <Internal>
      <SocketReceiveBufferSize min="64 kB" max="16 MB"/>
      <SocketSendBufferSize min="64 kB" max="16 MB"/>
    </Internal>
    <SharedMemory>
      <Enable>true</Enable>
      <LogLevel>warn</LogLevel>
    </SharedMemory>
  </Domain>
</CycloneDDS>
